xmodel/sol-approaches.log:7651:./SHEET6.md:14:- **(A) Żołądek's Newton–Puiseux charts** (reconstructed from zbMATH + Nguyen Van Chau arXiv:0804.3172v2 + MIMUW seminar abstract): resolve (f,g) at infinity in BOTH source and target; near each divisor use multivalued charts (x^{−1/m}, ξ) — truncated fractional-power series x(Σc_k x^{−k/m} + ξx^{−n/m}) where ξ replaces the Puiseux tail ("left and right Newton-Puiseux charts", Chau). The Keller map becomes near-monomial in charts; jac≡1 forces rigid exponent+leading-coefficient relations. Topological degree = sheets over infinity = Σ local degrees over dicritical divisors, with Orevkov's identity Σ(ramification excess at infinity) = td − 1. Case analysis = finite enumeration of dicritical/chart configurations for td ≤ 5; each killed by exponent arithmetic or analytic contradiction.
xmodel/sol-approaches.log:7653:./SHEET6.md:19:- **Orevkov/Domrina (3,4) were quasi-topological**: splice/Eisenbud–Neumann diagrams of the resolution boundary, homology, intersection form, canonical class K̃ = F*K + B of the branched cover; leaf kills by unimodularity/negative-definiteness/Abhyankar–Moh determinant arithmetic. Case tree at 4: ~19 configurations → 8 final diagrams (Domrina 2000), a few dozen leaves.
xmodel/sol-approaches.log:7654:./SHEET6.md:20:- **The 4→5 explosion is NOT case count — it is a provable method failure.** Egorov 2002 constructed a proper 5-sheeted "exotic covering" of C² (branching along two planes F₁*,F₂*; H²_c ≅ Z²; S₁² = −3, S₂² = −15, S₁·S₂ = −3; K(S₁)=K(S₂)=1) consistent with EVERY quasi-topological invariant Domrina–Orevkov use. So 5 required a new analytic layer: both 2008 proofs inject Abhyankar approximate-root/Puiseux-coefficient arithmetic (pole orders of g along f-branches; jac=1 exponent identities) that sees strictly more than the topology. That is the conceptual jump; the case tree itself only grew ~2×.
xmodel/sol-approaches.log:7655:./SHEET6.md:21:- **The real wall is at 9, not 6**: Sigray exhibits a decorated Eggers–Wall tree satisfying ALL his constraints at td = 9 with (deg f, deg g) = (48,64) — Moh's exceptional case — matching Orevkov's holomorphic "counterexamples to JC at infinity" (minimal td 9, bidegree (48,64); DO-I: the local-at-infinity method can at best reach ~8). Sigray verbatim: the problem at higher degree is not that computations are "more involved" but that a consistent tree EXISTS at 9. Corollary: td ∈ {6,7,8} is the entire remaining runway for at-infinity methods, with no known consistency obstruction below 9.
