xmodel/sol-g5-emission.log:2090:./SHEET6.md:8:- **Obtained (free, in `refs/`)**: Orevkov 1986 3-sheet paper (`jc86.pdf`, author-hosted English), Domrina–Orevkov 1998 4-sheet I (`do.pdf`), and — the decisive find — **Sigray's full 66-page 2008 ELTE PhD thesis "Jacobian trees and their applications"** (`sigray_full.pdf`, teo.elte.hu/minosites/ertekezes2008/sigray_i.pdf; booklet `sigray_i.pdf`), advisor A. Némethi.
xmodel/sol-g5-emission.log:2094:./SHEET6.md:21:- **The real wall is at 9, not 6**: Sigray exhibits a decorated Eggers–Wall tree satisfying ALL his constraints at td = 9 with (deg f, deg g) = (48,64) — Moh's exceptional case — matching Orevkov's holomorphic "counterexamples to JC at infinity" (minimal td 9, bidegree (48,64); DO-I: the local-at-infinity method can at best reach ~8). Sigray verbatim: the problem at higher degree is not that computations are "more involved" but that a consistent tree EXISTS at 9. Corollary: td ∈ {6,7,8} is the entire remaining runway for at-infinity methods, with no known consistency obstruction below 9.
